Z||000000||moonie||08-31-2000||09:59 PM||||can someone please tell me the actual purpose of the sail panel ?<P>i took 1 side off just to see what was behind it & it fell to pieces. instead of spending 150 dollars for new ones , i'm <BR>wondering could i just fill up the little <BR>snap in holes & paint that area. <BR>or , do i actually need plastic sail panels for some reason ? <P>i would like my car to remain stock & will eventually break down & spend the money for new ones , but smoothed & painted would be better than nothing for now . <P>thanks <BR>Moonie||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||08-31-2000||11:35 PM||||If this is on a notchback, you need the space behind for venting.  If you'll notice on the body of the Fiero, there is a vent (roughly centered and about 2/3 of the way up.  If you fill this in, you'll lose the vent (you need it).  I don't see any reason why you would pay $150 for new notchback sail panels.  You can get the frames at any boneyard for next to nothing and brand new panels from Paul McKibben for less than $50/pair, I think.<P>If you have a fastback, I don't know.||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||09-01-2000||12:06 AM||||I just sent you an email Moonie, check it out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||moonie||09-01-2000||12:08 AM||||notchback<P>i'm not talking about the vent holes than run from top to bottom .<BR>i have 2 tiny little holes that it looks like the plastic part that has SE on it <BR>snapped in. <BR>still , 50 bucks is 50 bucks ...<BR>do i actually NEED the plastic piece that has SE on it ????????????<BR>does it serve a purpose or is it just cosmetic ?<P>moonie <P>||||reg||1
